  "text": "Let's look at the given infinite product for defining (x_n) sequence:\n\\[x_1 = 2\\]\n\nThe sequence definition is:\n\\[x_k = \\frac{1}{x_{k-1}} + x_{k-1}\\]\n    \n\nNow let's reason through the product (sequence):\n1. For \\(n=1\\):\n\\[x_1 = 2\\]\n\\[1 + \\frac{1}{x_1} = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2}\\]\n\n2. For \\(n=2\\):\n\\[x_2 = \\frac{1}{x_1} + x_1 = 2 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2}\\]\n\\[1 + \\frac{1}{x_2} = 1 + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{5}{3}\\]\n\n3. For \\(n=3\\):\n\\[x_3 = \\frac{1}{x_2} + x_2 = \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{13}{6}\\]\n\\[1 + \\frac{1}{x_3} = 1 + \\frac{6}{13} = \\frac{19}{13}\\]\n\nHere, we see a pattern:\n\\[1 + \\frac{1}{x_n} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{4 - \\frac{1}{2^{n-1}}}{2^{n-1}} \\right)\\]\n\nOur final product is written:\n\\[\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right). = \\prod_\\frac{1}{2}^{\\infty} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot2^n \\right) = \\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} 2^n \\]\n\nSo let's acknowledge a couple of sums:\n\n\\[\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} 2^n = ? \\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} 2^n\\]\n\nBut, in this case,\n\\[ \\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} 2^n = (2)^{\\infty} = 2^{\\infty} = 2\\]\n\nTherefore the final result is:\n\\[\\boxed{2}\\]",
